1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,070
Several animals are jumping at once.

2
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:08,170
In this Ultimate Chicken Horse clip, who are you watching?

3
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:14,830
Follow the chicken. Will it avoid the saw and reach the next
platform? You start looking for different actions in the same view.

4
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:16,530
Now watch Gang Beasts.

5
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:22,890
Choose one of the bodies grabbing and hanging on,
and look for where that character is still holding.

6
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:25,130
These short shots show different situations.

7
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:27,830
Follow the next action of the character you chose.

8
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:31,005
Next is the developer’s early truck-level gameplay.

9
00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:34,110
Characters move and grapple on two trucks.

10
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:35,970
Pick one to follow in your mind.

11
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:39,370
Choosing whom to watch does not change the rules.

12
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:43,530
It changes which movement you look for
first and what you want to see happen next.

13
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:47,905
Using footage from these two actual games, we will separate recognition

14
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:51,610
cues, a reason to care, and a view that makes important moments readable.

15
00:00:55,917 --> 00:00:59,522
Separate three tasks: recognizing a participant, caring

16
00:00:59,537 --> 00:01:02,847
about them, and understanding their current situation.

17
00:01:03,017 --> 00:01:07,487
You may recognize the red character without having a reason to follow it.

18
00:01:07,657 --> 00:01:11,287
Or you may want to cheer for a friend but be unable to find them on screen.

19
00:01:11,417 --> 00:01:14,747
A larger name tag can help with recognition.

20
00:01:14,957 --> 00:01:19,107
It does not automatically provide motivation or explain the danger in the match.

21
00:01:19,337 --> 00:01:24,942
When designing a spectator view, first look for breaks between
these three tasks before adding elaborate cheering effects.

22
00:01:24,957 --> 00:01:29,642
Let us watch the two games in action, finding a participant,

23
00:01:29,657 --> 00:01:33,967
choosing whom to root for, and reading the risk and outcome.

24
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:38,050
Watch this Ultimate Chicken Horse developer recording.

25
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:42,790
A robot-costumed monkey and a rabbit wearing a hat differ in shape.

26
00:01:42,860 --> 00:01:45,090
Look for those differences as they jump.

27
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:51,170
Appearance helps you find the same animal after
the group separates toward different platforms.

28
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:54,710
Remembering only its screen position can become confusing.

29
00:01:54,980 --> 00:01:57,350
Now watch the Gang Beasts truck level.

30
00:01:57,560 --> 00:02:01,070
In this early version, the bodies have similar shapes but different colors.

31
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:03,065
Follow the cyan character.

32
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:09,310
As characters approach each other and the camera
turns, remembering the left side is not enough.

33
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:12,650
Also track which truck and which nearby character connect to the participant.

34
00:02:12,860 --> 00:02:16,230
A body moving below the roof does not immediately end the action.

35
00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:18,310
Look for a hand still at the edge.

36
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:21,690
Reading an identity and following its action are different tasks.

37
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:23,690
This is not a controlled experiment.

38
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:28,630
We are observing what clues appearance, color and surrounding positions offer.

39
00:02:28,820 --> 00:02:32,105
Check whether the same participant can be found on a small screen or in a crowd.

40
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:35,030
A marker must be readable with the moving body.

41
00:02:35,767 --> 00:02:40,057
Adding identity information does not require filling the screen with name tags.

42
00:02:40,307 --> 00:02:43,977
Choose cues that remain readable alongside the action.

43
00:02:44,187 --> 00:02:48,197
Body shape, a short name and an emblem are different cues.

44
00:02:48,347 --> 00:02:51,457
They should consistently point to the same participant.

45
00:02:51,707 --> 00:02:55,052
If a side card shows a star while the character carries a

46
00:02:55,067 --> 00:02:58,777
moon, additional information can create more confusion.

47
00:02:59,027 --> 00:03:02,012
Check wide camera views and crowded moments too.

48
00:03:02,027 --> 00:03:06,237
A useful marker helps people find their participant
again when the scene becomes difficult to read.

49
00:03:06,527 --> 00:03:09,297
Avoid oversized markers that cover important movement.

50
00:03:09,647 --> 00:03:13,367
Helping viewers identify someone should not hide what that person is doing.

51
00:03:14,083 --> 00:03:16,453
Now watch the window-cleaning platforms.

52
00:03:16,603 --> 00:03:19,313
Choose red or yellow.

53
00:03:19,743 --> 00:03:23,673
Standing on the platform and hanging below
it lead to different immediate actions.

54
00:03:24,003 --> 00:03:27,508
Following yellow can raise the question of getting back up;

55
00:03:27,523 --> 00:03:30,913
following red can raise the question of maintaining its grip.

56
00:03:31,263 --> 00:03:33,693
The same view supports different questions.

57
00:03:33,943 --> 00:03:35,873
Now watch another attempt on the trucks.

58
00:03:36,283 --> 00:03:40,993
Cyan and yellow come together while green moves nearby.

59
00:03:41,263 --> 00:03:43,628
Look first for the hands and body of the participant you chose.

60
00:03:43,643 --> 00:03:48,473
A body tilting while held can give a viewer a reason to hope it stays aboard.

61
00:03:48,843 --> 00:03:53,653
The visible posture offers a question about the
next move without a long character biography.

62
00:03:54,063 --> 00:03:56,433
You can choose a different character in the next attempt.

63
00:03:56,883 --> 00:04:01,193
This means deciding whom to follow, not adding a new game feature.

64
00:04:01,463 --> 00:04:04,073
Do not infer a player’s personality from one action.

65
00:04:04,443 --> 00:04:06,993
Start by describing the movement you can actually see.

66
00:04:07,343 --> 00:04:10,608
The view can provide room for a small expectation.

67
00:04:10,623 --> 00:04:15,023
Whether people actually enjoy watching more
still needs to be checked with viewers.

68
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:23,385
People choose whom to support for different reasons: an appealing

69
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:27,290
appearance, a familiar play style, or someone they know.

70
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:31,485
A first-time spectator need not memorize every contestant's biography.

71
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:34,970
Start with a small piece of information connected to the action on screen.

72
00:04:35,220 --> 00:04:38,325
Distinguishing a cautious route from a risky attempt

73
00:04:38,340 --> 00:04:41,670
gives viewers something specific to look for next.

74
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:44,670
This example does not require staking points or paying anything.

75
00:04:44,700 --> 00:04:50,890
The choice simply selects a participant to
follow and changes no prize or property.

76
00:04:51,340 --> 00:04:54,770
A selection screen does not prove that people care.

77
00:04:55,220 --> 00:04:59,570
Readable action and an actual desire to keep
watching still need separate evaluation.

78
00:05:00,550 --> 00:05:02,860
Return to the window-cleaning scaffold.

79
00:05:03,130 --> 00:05:06,860
A body can hang below while a hand remains attached to the edge.

80
00:05:06,990 --> 00:05:10,700
Watch the relationship between the hand and platform.

81
00:05:11,010 --> 00:05:14,420
The open space below makes the danger legible.

82
00:05:15,030 --> 00:05:21,160
A gripping hand and the next foothold may matter
more to this moment than a large set of statistics.

83
00:05:21,310 --> 00:05:24,640
Switch to early Ultimate Chicken Horse gameplay.

84
00:05:24,910 --> 00:05:28,755
Watch the moving saw and the white character jumping.

85
00:05:28,770 --> 00:05:32,000
The danger lies between takeoff and the next platform.

86
00:05:32,350 --> 00:05:34,460
The horse moves in a different attempt.

87
00:05:34,830 --> 00:05:39,620
The cut should not make its outcome look like a
continuation of the white character’s earlier action.

88
00:05:40,190 --> 00:05:43,080
The same relationship appears at the side of a truck.

89
00:05:43,550 --> 00:05:49,160
Seeing the roof, hanging body and road
below helps explain why that grip matters.

90
00:05:49,530 --> 00:05:51,580
The next attempt brings them together on the roof again.

91
00:05:51,830 --> 00:05:54,440
Follow who holds whom and which edge they approach.

92
00:05:54,790 --> 00:05:59,680
A tight crop can lose the next space, while
a very wide view can lose hands and bodies.

93
00:05:59,870 --> 00:06:03,410
Check whether the participant and immediate risk remain connected in the view.

94
00:06:07,483 --> 00:06:12,113
After establishing whom to watch, show which moments matter to that participant.

95
00:06:12,323 --> 00:06:15,553
A name and ranking do not explain the current action.

96
00:06:15,603 --> 00:06:19,208
Across a narrow platform, distance and landing matter. Near the

97
00:06:19,223 --> 00:06:22,993
finish, the goal and a competitor's position may become more relevant.

98
00:06:23,163 --> 00:06:25,433
The useful information changes with the situation.

99
00:06:25,563 --> 00:06:30,253
Giving every statistic equal space can shrink
the movement viewers actually need to see.

100
00:06:30,503 --> 00:06:34,428
Our diagram first connects the participant, obstacle and goal.

101
00:06:34,443 --> 00:06:38,673
Check those relationships in the actual view before adding more information.

102
00:06:38,843 --> 00:06:42,793
The camera's emphasis should agree with the actual state.

103
00:06:42,983 --> 00:06:46,393
Do not present an unfinished contest as if its outcome were already settled.

104
00:06:47,167 --> 00:06:51,752
In this space-themed Ultimate Chicken Horse gameplay, choose a moving

105
00:06:51,767 --> 00:06:56,737
animal. Follow its rising body, propulsion flames and nearby rocks together.

106
00:06:57,007 --> 00:06:59,977
The framing changes as the animals spread apart.

107
00:07:00,207 --> 00:07:05,437
Seeing the overall space and quickly finding the
animal you were following are different tasks.

108
00:07:05,787 --> 00:07:08,417
Look for the same appearance in the next view.

109
00:07:08,627 --> 00:07:13,337
Watching only the center because the animal was
there earlier can make you follow someone else.

110
00:07:13,547 --> 00:07:17,152
Flames beneath the feet and nearby rocks help reveal movement direction.

111
00:07:17,167 --> 00:07:22,117
After recognizing the participant, read where
it is going and what it will encounter next.

112
00:07:22,367 --> 00:07:25,317
Distinguish approaching the flag from rising again.

113
00:07:25,427 --> 00:07:28,877
Edited attempts should not be read as one continuous flight.

114
00:07:29,107 --> 00:07:32,392
We are observing visible movement and framing, not discovering

115
00:07:32,407 --> 00:07:36,297
the internal camera algorithm or the best view for everyone.

116
00:07:36,487 --> 00:07:39,717
Check whether the chosen participant can be found after a framing change.

117
00:07:39,867 --> 00:07:42,687
Even an attractive view should preserve the next action.

118
00:07:43,417 --> 00:07:46,147
A small choice can be an entry point into watching.

119
00:07:46,337 --> 00:07:51,167
If the selection process becomes longer or more complicated
than the contest, it can interrupt the experience.

120
00:07:51,397 --> 00:07:57,087
Offer enough information for an unfamiliar viewer to choose,
while considering options to simply watch or switch later.

121
00:07:57,697 --> 00:08:01,482
Separate two testing questions: can someone locate the participant

122
00:08:01,497 --> 00:08:04,987
they support, and do they actually want to continue watching?

123
00:08:05,317 --> 00:08:08,767
Screen checks and input records can answer parts of the first question.

124
00:08:08,957 --> 00:08:12,422
The second requires showing it to people and examining their responses.

125
00:08:12,437 --> 00:08:16,667
Passing an automated test does not establish greater engagement.

126
00:08:16,877 --> 00:08:20,457
Distinguish a comprehension aid from evidence about enjoyment.

127
00:08:21,183 --> 00:08:24,833
Finally, watch cyan and yellow on the truck.

128
00:08:25,043 --> 00:08:30,433
Follow the sequence of grappling, a body
tilting, and the distance between them changing.

129
00:08:30,723 --> 00:08:34,108
The standing position alone does not settle the outcome.

130
00:08:34,123 --> 00:08:38,933
A character may rise after falling over or keep
a hand attached after moving below the roof.

131
00:08:39,203 --> 00:08:45,033
When both bodies descend at the edge, follow which contacts remain.

132
00:08:45,283 --> 00:08:49,073
A large label for the favored participant
cannot communicate that process by itself.

133
00:08:49,303 --> 00:08:51,168
Next is a separate scaffold scene.

134
00:08:51,183 --> 00:08:54,593
Watch how the hanging characters and platform angle change.

135
00:08:54,963 --> 00:08:57,093
Follow the red character through the shot.

136
00:08:57,403 --> 00:09:00,673
Distinguishing a remaining grip from a falling body connects

137
00:09:01,183 --> 00:09:04,473
the visible outcome with the action that produced it.

138
00:09:04,943 --> 00:09:07,853
This material does not establish the whole match’s winner.

139
00:09:08,163 --> 00:09:10,213
We describe only the attempts and falls shown.

140
00:09:10,543 --> 00:09:15,753
Connect the selected participant, the action
attempted and the outcome actually verified.

141
00:09:15,983 --> 00:09:19,013
That connection should be readable before adding a large call to cheer.

142
00:09:19,833 --> 00:09:24,798
Ask four questions about your spectator view: who am I watching, why

143
00:09:24,813 --> 00:09:30,103
did I choose them, what matters now, and what actually happened?

144
00:09:30,173 --> 00:09:35,338
Appearance and markers identify the participant.
A brief introduction and visible behavior

145
00:09:35,353 --> 00:09:40,083
provide a reason to care. The camera and result
display can help maintain that connection.

146
00:09:40,313 --> 00:09:42,843
Do not stop at enlarging one element.

147
00:09:42,873 --> 00:09:48,118
If viewers lose the character beneath an attractive name
tag, return to the actual movement and check again.

148
00:09:48,133 --> 00:09:52,903
Keep readability checks separate from evaluations of enjoyment.

149
00:09:53,173 --> 00:09:56,123
Actual spectator responses require showing
the experience to people and listening.

150
00:09:56,473 --> 00:10:00,303
A useful starting point for making other people's play worth watching

151
00:10:00,493 --> 00:10:03,983
is helping viewers follow the actions of someone they care about.
